<div><span>1 year</span><p>to file a TWIA windstorm claim from the date of damage</p></div>
<div><span>61 days</span><p>written notice required before suing over a weather claim (§542A.003)</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Why earlier is easier</h2>
<p>The reason timing matters is evidence. Every week after a loss, some of it disappears. A tarp goes over the damaged slope. Wet drywall and carpet pad get torn out and hauled off. The roofer replaces the roof and the old shingles go in a dumpster. Damaged furniture goes to the curb. None of that is wrong; you have a duty to prevent further damage. But it means whoever documents the loss first has the best record. Before anything is removed, photograph it, measure it, and keep a sample of damaged materials where you can. Our <a href="/tools/contents-inventory-builder/">contents inventory builder</a> helps you list belongings room by room before they are thrown out.</p>

<h2>What a public adjuster can still do at each stage</h2>

<h3>Before you file</h3>
<p>This is the best time. We can read the policy before anything is said to the insurer, document the damage before temporary repairs cover it, and make sure the claim is reported in a way that matches the policy's conditions. Emergency mitigation (tarping, water extraction) should not wait for anyone; take photos and keep the receipts. Our <a href="/post/how-to-document-damage-for-your-claim/">documentation guide</a> covers what to capture first.</p>

<h3>After the inspection, before the offer</h3>
<p>Still early. The insurer's adjuster has seen the property, but the estimate is not final. A public adjuster can submit an independent estimate and supporting photos before the carrier settles on its number, and can request a re-inspection if areas were skipped, such as the attic, the north slopes or the wet drywall behind the cabinets.</p>

<h3>After a low offer or partial approval</h3>
<p>This is when most people call us, and it is a normal time to hire. Under §542.056 the insurer generally has 15 business days after receiving everything it asked for to accept or reject the claim. Once you have its estimate, the dispute becomes concrete: which line items, which slopes, which rooms. A public adjuster compares the two estimates line by line and presents what is missing. See <a href="/post/hail-damage-supplement-claim/">supplement claims</a> for how missed items are added.</p>

<h3>After you have been paid</h3>
<p>Accepting a check does not, by itself, close the door. Many property claims can be supplemented or reopened when damage was missed or hidden damage turns up during repairs. Two things to check first:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li><strong>Did you sign a release?</strong> A claim check is usually not a release, but a signed settlement agreement or release can end the claim. If you signed anything beyond the check endorsement, have an attorney read it.</li>
<li><strong>Is recoverable depreciation still pending?</strong> On replacement cost policies, the insurer often pays actual cash value first and holds back depreciation until repairs are done. Many policies set a time limit for completing repairs and claiming it. Check your policy's loss settlement section.</li>
</ul>

<h3>After a denial</h3>
<p>A denial is not always the last word. Many denials rest on a factual finding, such as "wear and tear," "no storm-created opening," or "damage predates the policy," that better evidence can answer. A public adjuster can document the cause and request reconsideration. Pulling <a href="/tools/storm-report-lookup/">NOAA storm reports</a> for your address and date is often a first step on hail and wind denials. If the denial is a pure coverage question (the policy simply excludes what happened, such as flood under a homeowners policy), more documentation will not change it, and an attorney may be the better call. See <a href="/post/denied-hail-damage-insurance-claim/">denied hail claims</a>.</p>

<h3>During appraisal</h3>
<p>Appraisal resolves disputes over the <em>amount</em> of loss, not coverage. Each side picks an appraiser and the two choose an umpire. If you have already invoked appraisal, a public adjuster may still help by preparing the detailed estimate and documentation your appraiser presents, but check what your policy and your appraiser's agreement allow. Read <a href="/post/appraisal-for-hail-damage-claim/">appraisal for hail damage claims</a> before invoking it.</p>

<h3>In litigation</h3>
<p>Once a lawsuit is filed, your attorney leads. A public adjuster generally will not take over a claim at this stage. If the attorney wants an independent damage estimate, we can work alongside them, but that is the attorney's decision.</p>

