<h2>The deadlines that actually close the door</h2>
<p>The stage of the claim matters less than these dates. Find them before anything else.</p>

<h3>Your policy's reporting and suit deadlines</h3>
<p>Every policy has a "Duties after loss" section that says how and when you must report damage, and most have a "Suit against us" condition that limits when you can sue. Many private Texas homeowners policies set a one-year (or similar) deadline to report wind and hail damage, but the wording varies. There is no single universal deadline for private policies, so read yours. We cover hail timelines in detail in <a href="/post/how-long-do-you-have-to-file-a-hail-claim-in-texas/">how long you have to file a hail claim in Texas</a>.</p>

<h3>TWIA claims on the coast</h3>
<p>Texas Windstorm Insurance Association claims must be filed within one year of the date of damage. The commissioner may extend that by up to 180 days for good cause, but do not count on it. TWIA generally has 60 days to accept or deny after it receives the claim and the information it requested. More in <a href="/services/hurricane-windstorm-claims/">hurricane and windstorm claims</a>.</p>

<h3>The §542A notice before a lawsuit</h3>
<div class="callout callout--law"><p><strong>Texas law:</strong> For weather-related property claims, you must give the insurer written notice at least 61 days before filing suit, stating the specific amount you claim is owed (Tex. Ins. Code §542A.003).</p></div>
<p>That notice needs a number, and the number needs support. A documented, line-item estimate is often the foundation for it, which is one reason public adjusters and attorneys frequently work together on weather claims headed toward a dispute. If you are getting close to your policy's suit deadline, talk to an attorney now; 61 days goes quickly.</p>

<h3>Deadlines that run in your favor</h3>
<p>Texas also sets deadlines for the insurer: 15 days to acknowledge and begin investigating, 15 business days after receiving requested items to decide, and 5 business days after acceptance to pay, with 15 extra days after a declared weather catastrophe (§§542.055 to 542.059). Missing them can trigger penalty interest and attorney's fees (§542.060). Our <a href="/tools/texas-claim-deadline-calculator/">deadline calculator</a> works out your dates, and <a href="/post/how-long-does-an-insurance-adjuster-have-to-contact-you/">how long an adjuster has to contact you</a> explains each one.</p>

<h2>What to send if you are calling late</h2>
<p>The further along the claim, the more paper matters. Have these ready:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>The full policy with all endorsements.</li>
<li>Every estimate, letter and email from the insurer, including any denial or partial denial.</li>
<li>Records of every payment, with dates and amounts.</li>
<li>Your photos and videos from before and after any repairs.</li>
<li>Contractor estimates and invoices for anything already repaired.</li>
<li>Any document you signed other than a check.</li>
</ul>
<p>Not sure whether your claim is worth representation at all at this point? Start with <a href="/post/should-i-use-a-public-adjuster/">should I use a public adjuster</a>.</p>
